Q: sql: scan row(s) with unknown number of columns (select * from ...) A: <p><a href="https://jmoiron.github.io/sqlx/#query" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>sqlx</code></a> does this with <code>StructScan</code>.</p>
<pre><code>type Place struct {
    Country       string
    City          sql.NullString
    TelephoneCode int `db:&quot;telcode&quot;`
}

rows, err := db.Queryx(&quot;SELECT * FROM place&quot;)
for rows.Next() {
    var p Place
    err = rows.StructScan(&amp;p)
}
</code></pre>
<p>Here is an example of how reflection works.</p>
<p>I often want to scan arbitrary columns into a struct without being constrained to a fixed list of fields to be scanned for each query.  Reflection gives your code runtime insight into data structures in memory, much like how you would do <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duck_typing" rel="nofollow noreferrer">duck typing</a> in JavaScript, for instance, or iterate over arbitrary keys in an object and check for the presence of a field.  Something that's more difficult in Go, but not impossible.  There are usually more idiomatic patterns, but it's much needed here.</p>
<p>First we create a function that can get a pointer to a struct field from an arbitrary column name.</p>
<pre><code>func GetStructFieldByName(s any, name string) (any, error) {
    v := reflect.ValueOf(s).Elem()
    if v.Kind() != reflect.Struct {
        return nil, errors.New(&quot;s is not a struct.&quot;)
    }

    var joinStr []string

    split := strings.Split(name, &quot;_&quot;)
    for _, s := range split {
        runes := []rune(s)
        upper := strings.ToUpper(string(runes[0])) + string(runes[1:])
        joinStr = append(joinStr, upper)
    }

    n := strings.Join(joinStr, &quot;&quot;)

    field := v.FieldByName(n)
    if !field.IsValid() {
        return nil,
            fmt.Errorf(&quot;Error retrieving struct field: %s&quot;, n)
    }

    return field.Addr().Interface(), nil
}
</code></pre>
<p>This function works with <code>underscore_named</code> columns mapped to <code>CapsCase</code> struct fields.</p>
<p>First it gets a <a href="https://pkg.go.dev/reflect#Value" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Value</code></a> type for <code>s</code>, expecting a pointer to a struct.  <code>Value</code> is an abstraction around types that enables reflection at runtime.</p>
<p><a href="https://pkg.go.dev/reflect#Value.Elem" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Elem()</code></a> unwraps the pointer into the type of the value it points to.</p>
<p><code>Value</code> has a method <a href="https://pkg.go.dev/reflect#Value.FieldByName" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>FieldByName()</code></a> that can reflect on a struct and get an actual reference to the named field at runtime.  Once we have the field, we call <code>field.</code><a href="https://pkg.go.dev/reflect#Value.Addr" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Addr()</code></a><code>.</code><a href="https://pkg.go.dev/reflect#Value.Interface" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Interface()</code></a> to get a plain <code>interface{}</code> pointing to it that may be used with <code>rows.Scan()</code>.</p>
<p>Next, a wrapper around it to iterate over a list of column names:</p>
<pre><code>func GetStructFieldsByNames(s any, names []string) (values []any, err error) {
    for _, n := range names {
        f, err := GetStructFieldByName(s, n)
        if err != nil {
            return values, err
        }

        values = append(values, f)
    }

    return values, nil
}
</code></pre>
<p>Now you can use it like this.</p>
<pre><code>var results []MyStruct

rows, err := db.Query(&quot;...&quot;, args...)
if err != nil {
    return fmt.Errorf(&quot;Error executing select query: %w&quot;, err)
}
defer rows.Close()
columns, err := rows.Columns()

for rows.Next() {
    dest := MyStruct{}
    v, err := GetStructFieldsByNames(&amp;dest, columns)
    if err != nil {
        return fmt.Errorf(&quot;Error getting fields: %w&quot;, err)
    }

    err = rows.Scan(v...)
    if err != nil {
        return fmt.Errorf(&quot;Error scanning: %w&quot;, err)
    }

    results = append(results, dest)
}
</code></pre>
<p>This seems to work well, although you will definitely have to test it or extend it for your use case.</p>
<p>From here, you could filter or transform the column names, or build arbitrary, dynamic queries on generic types that return an unknown amount of columns at runtime.</p>
<pre><code>type Where struct {
    Column string
    Values []any
}

type Query[T any] struct {
    Stmt string
    Args []any
}

func CreateSelectQuery[T any](table string, fields []string, where []Where, order string,
    desc bool, limit int, offset int) (q Query[T], err error) {
    if len(fields) == 0 {
        return q, errors.New(&quot;No fields provided to build select statement.&quot;)
    }

    q.Stmt = &quot;SELECT &quot;
    q.Stmt = q.Stmt + strings.Join(fields, &quot;, &quot;)
    q.Stmt = q.Stmt + &quot; FROM &quot; + table

    if len(where) &gt; 0 {
        q.Stmt = q.Stmt + &quot; WHERE &quot;

        var whereParts []string

        for _, w := range where {
            argP := []string{}

            // Build slice of arg placeholders for each id
            for range w.Values {
                argP = append(argP, &quot;?&quot;)
            }

            // Create expanded argStr of placeholders joined by commas
            argStr := strings.Join(argP, &quot;, &quot;)

            // Create full WHERE clause with expanded argStr
            whereParts = append(whereParts, fmt.Sprintf(&quot;%s IN (%v)&quot;, w.Column, argStr))

            // Append ids to list of output arguments for use in query
            q.Args = append(q.Args, w.Values...)
        }

        q.Stmt = q.Stmt + strings.Join(whereParts, &quot; AND &quot;)
    }

    if order != &quot;&quot; {
        q.Stmt = q.Stmt + fmt.Sprintf(&quot; ORDER BY %s&quot;, order)
        if desc {
            q.Stmt = q.Stmt + &quot; DESC&quot;
        }
    }

    if limit != 0 {
        q.Stmt = q.Stmt + &quot; LIMIT ?&quot;
        q.Args = append(q.Args, limit)
    }

    if offset != 0 {
        q.Stmt = q.Stmt + &quot; OFFSET ?&quot;
        q.Args = append(q.Args, offset)
    }

    return q, nil
}
</code></pre>
<p>This creates a <code>Query</code> with a field <code>Stmt</code> that contains the statement we build clause by clause.  We build a sidecar array of <code>Args</code> in parallel so that we have a fully constructed query and an array of args in order that can be <code>spread...</code> into the call to <code>db.Query()</code> for replacement with the placeholder <code>?</code>s.</p>
<p>You may wish to slim down the creation function and assign those query options to the struct as needed.</p>
<p>To me <code>StructScan</code> seems like something that should be built-in.  Rows and scanning are very cumbersome, and ORMs can be too much.  I like sqlx's thin solution on top of the standard sql interface.</p>

Q: DO-WHILE loop challenge A: <pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>using static System.Console;

string? actualPassword = &quot;ludo5656&quot;;
string? password;

int maxAttempts = 3;

do
{
    Write(&quot;Enter your password: &quot;);
    password = ReadLine();

    if (password != actualPassword)
    maxAttempts -= 1;
    if (maxAttempts == 0)
    {
    WriteLine(&quot;Maximum attempts reached!&quot;);
    break;
    }
}
while (password != actualPassword);

if (password == actualPassword)
    WriteLine(&quot;Correct!&quot;);
</code></pre>
<p>Thank you, even though it works, is there a better solution that you can think of ?</p>
